  data:  # 3681
    kind: invariant  # 3681
    title: "symbol_path_slot assumes the NAME slot is colon-free; objc selectors and rust '::' paths corrupt the module hint into the rejecting branch, silently dropping the finding"  # 3681
    status: violated  # 3681
    priority: 1  # 3681
    parent:  # 3681
    tags:  # 3681
    - taint  # 3681
    - io_boundary  # 3681
    - analysis_quality  # 3681
    - adr_0036  # 3681
    - objc  # 3681
    - rust  # 3681
    isbefore: []  # 3681
    duplicate_of: []  # 3681
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 3681
    pr_ref:  # 3681
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-06 while filling INV-linub's unmeasured go/rust/objc cells.\nRead in code AND reproduced with two passing positive controls, so the failures\nare evidence rather than a broken harness.\n\nTHE DEFECT. ir.symbol_path_slot parses `{lang}:{path}:{span}:{name}:{kind}` as\n\n    return \":\".join(parts[1:-3])\n\nwhich is right-anchored on the assumption its own docstring states outright:\n\"the path slot is the one colon-TOLERANT slot in the grammar while\nlang/span/name/kind are colon-free.\" THE NAME SLOT IS NOT COLON-FREE. Two\nlanguages emit colon-bearing NAMES today:\n\n    objc   selectors            createFileAtPath:contents:attributes:\n    rust   qualified path calls  std::fs::create_dir\n\nWhen the name carries colons, `parts[1:-3]` over-consumes rightward out of the\npath slot and into the name, returning a string that is neither.\n\nREPRODUCTION (positive controls first, per L9):\n\n  PASS  python:os:0-0:mkdir:unresolved\n          want 'os'                     got 'os'\n  PASS  rust:std::fs:0-0:write:external_symbol      <- the colon-bearing PATH\n          want 'std::fs'                got 'std::fs'   case the docstring cites\n  FAIL  rust:external:0-0:std::fs::create_dir:unresolved\n          want 'external'               got 'external:0-0:std::fs'\n  FAIL  objc:external:0-0:createFileAtPath:contents:attributes::unresolved\n          want 'external'               got 'external:0-0:createFileAtPath:contents'\n\ntaint._extract_callee_NAME gets the same ids RIGHT (it anchors on the span token\nby finding the digit-and-dash part, then takes everything between span and kind).\nSo the two parsers disagree on the same string, and the module one is the wrong\none. That disagreement is the tell.\n\nWHY IT IS FATAL RATHER THAN LOSSY. _lookup_named_entry branches on the module\nhint:\n\n    if module_hint and module_hint not in _UNRESOLVED_MODULE_PLACEHOLDERS:\n        ... return the first hit whose module matches, else None\n\n'external' IS a placeholder, so a correct parse degrades to short-name matching\nplus the F3 gate -- the intended fails-soft path. 'external:0-0:std::fs' is NOT\na placeholder, so the corrupted value falls into the module-FILTER branch and is\ncompared as though it were a real module name. It matches nothing, and the\nfinding is dropped SILENTLY.\n\nTHIS IS THE THIRD TIME THIS EXACT FAILURE HAS BEEN FIXED. The code comments\nrecord the other two:\n  * _extract_callee_module used to take parts[1], truncating 'std::fs' to 'std'\n    -- \"an edge that correctly named std::fs was refused while an edge carrying\n    no module at all matched\" (L50, per symbol_path_slot's own docstring);\n  * _lookup_named_entry was missing '<external>' from the placeholder set, so it\n    \"fell into the module-FILTER branch below and was compared as though it were\n    a real module name -- matching nothing and silently dropping the finding.\"\nSame sink, same branch, third door. That is what makes this structural and not a\none-line typo: the grammar assumes ONE colon-tolerant slot and the emitters use\nTWO.\n\nEXPOSURE, stated as catalogue shape rather than as a cohort number I have not\nmeasured:\n  * objc -- 56 of 62 sinks (90%) are method-kind, and objc method names ARE\n    selectors, so essentially every objc sink id that carries a selector name is\n    parsed wrong. The objc analyzer emits exactly this shape today; the id above\n    was taken verbatim from a live run.\n  * rust -- all nine catalogued rust sink modules are colon-bearing, and rust's\n    catalogue is one of only THREE marked `status: complete` (with python and\n    erlang), so this lands on the surface most likely to be trusted.\n  * No other language emits colon-bearing names today, so the blast radius is\n    objc + rust unless a new analyzer adopts the shape.\n  NOT MEASURED: how many edges in a real repo carry a colon-bearing name. The\n  cohort holds no objc, and rust's exposure depends on how often the analyzer\n  emits a fully-qualified call name rather than a bare one. Size before fixing.\n\nRUST HAS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT BREAK -- do not assume this fix alone makes rust\nreachable. The analyzer emits the callee name as `std::fs::create_dir` (colon\nseparator), while the catalogue's qualified key is built as module + \".\" + name\n= `std::fs.create_dir` (dot separator). The index therefore holds 'create_dir'\nand 'std::fs.create_dir' but not 'std::fs::create_dir', so even a correctly\nparsed 'external' module hint leaves the qualified name unmatchable. Both breaks\nmust close before any rust catalogue entry is reachable through a qualified call.\n\nDIRECTION (mandatory under INV-linub). Fixing this moves module_hint from\ngarbage to 'external', which moves the lookup from the REJECTING module-filter\nbranch to the DEGRADING placeholder branch. That means MORE matches, not fewer.\nFor sinks that raises recall. But the same index is used for SANITIZERS, and a\nnewly-matching sanitizer registers a barrier that can earn `sanitized` and DROP\na flow (#214). So this change can move findings in both directions and must be\nmeasured on findings, not on match counts."  # 3681
    fields:  # 3681
      statement: "For any symbol id, symbol_path_slot returns the PATH slot only, regardless of whether the NAME slot contains colons; a callee whose analyzer emitted no module information yields the 'external' placeholder and therefore degrades to short-name matching rather than being compared against a corrupted module string."  # 3681
      root_cause: "ir.symbol_path_slot (packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ir.py:1394-1397) returns ':'.join(parts[1:-3]), which is right-anchored on the documented assumption that the path slot is the only colon-tolerant slot. The NAME slot is also colon-tolerant in practice: objc emits selectors (createFileAtPath:contents:attributes:) and rust emits qualified path calls (std::fs::create_dir). When the name carries colons the slice over-consumes rightward out of the path and into the name. taint._extract_callee_name parses the same ids correctly by anchoring on the span token, so the two parsers disagree on the same string. The corrupted value is not in _UNRESOLVED_MODULE_PLACEHOLDERS, so _lookup_named_entry takes the module-FILTER branch, which treats a present-but-mismatched module as a rejection."  # 3681
